1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,832
How does the card zone know a call really comes from ecom?

2
00:00:02,882 --> 00:00:11,400
Entering an office building, you show an ID card from the building's own pass office, and reception gives you a day pass that lists the floors you may visit.

3
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:14,280
The card zone does the same for every call.

4
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:18,960
The first proof is a certificate signed by the card zone's own pass office.

5
00:00:19,010 --> 00:00:24,192
Both sides show one, so each knows who is on the other end: mutual TLS.

6
00:00:24,242 --> 00:00:28,752
The second proof is an access token from the card zone's own login server.

7
00:00:28,802 --> 00:00:32,256
Inside the token are roles: the floors you may visit.

8
00:00:32,306 --> 00:00:40,488
Each operation needs its own role, so even the real ecom can only do what its roles allow: bind a card, read it, or charge it.

9
00:00:40,538 --> 00:00:43,056
Steal one proof, and you still get nothing.

10
00:00:43,106 --> 00:00:50,976
The card zone keeps its own pass office and its own login server, separate from ecom's, so nothing outside the room can hand out its passes.

11
00:00:51,026 --> 00:00:54,432
Next: how the card zone talks to every card network.
